{"answer":"Using **Taylor's Theorem** for expansion about $x$:\n$$\\tan^{-1}(x+h) = \\tan^{-1}(x) + h \\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan^{-1}x) + \\frac{h^2}{2!} \\frac{d^2}{dx^2}(\\tan^{-1}x) + \\dots$$\n$$= \\tan^{-1}(x) + h \\frac{1}{1+x^2} - h^2 \\frac{x}{(1+x^2)^2} + \\dots$$\n\nTo find $\\tan^{-1}(1.003)$, let $x = 1$ and $h = 0.003$:\n$$\\tan^{-1}(1 + 0.003) = \\tan^{-1}(1) + (0.003)\\left(\\frac{1}{1+1^2}\\right) - \\frac{(0.003)^2}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{(1+1^2)^2}\\right) + \\dots$$\n$$\\tan^{-1}(1.003) = \\frac{\\pi}{4} + (0.003)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) - \\frac{0.000009}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) + \\dots$$\nSubstitute $\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\approx 0.785398$:\n$$\\tan^{-1}(1.003) = 0.785398 + 0.001500 - 0.000001125$$\n$$\\tan^{-1}(1.003) = 0.786896875 \\approx 0.78690$$","proTip":"Keep extra decimal places during intermediate steps to ensure accuracy up to the required 5 decimal places.","videoLinks":[{"title":"Taylor series expansion tan inverse","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Taylor+series+expansion+tan+inverse"},{"title":"numerical evaluation using Taylor series","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=numerical+evaluation+using+Taylor+series"}]}